acme-devops: Hey team — our ingestion job started throwing 429s ~15m ago. We're on the free tier (org: acme-inc, acct: acct_9X7). This is a spike from a demo load (bursty), we need short-term relief while we either backoff or upgrade. Error payload below. Any chance for a temporary burst allowance or fast upgrade path?

acme-devops: Response: HTTP 429 Too Many Requests
{
  "error": "rate_limit_exceeded",
  "message": "Rate limit exceeded: tokens_per_minute"
}
Headers: x-rate-limit-limit: 60, x-rate-limit-remaining: 0, x-rate-limit-reset: 24

maria_support: Sorry you're hitting this — I can help. Can you paste the exact curl you used + the timestamped response headers (if available)? Also: is this hitting the generation API or embeddings?

acme-devops: Generation (text completion). Curl we used: 
```bash
curl https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate -H "Authorization: Bearer sk_test_acme" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"model":"rw-large-v1","prompt":"...","max_tokens":512}'
```
Response headers from our capture (epoch seconds):
- 1791012300: x-rate-limit-limit=60, x-rate-limit-remaining=0, x-rate-limit-reset=1791012360
- 1791012310: 429 body + x-request-id=req_83b9

maria_support: Thanks — that's helpful. Two immediate options: 1) short-term exception (temporary burst allowance) we can request from billing/ops; 2) upgrade to paid tier which raises per-minute token caps — sales can assist. I'll open an escalation.

maria_support: @nora_billing, can you check acct_9X7 for eligibility for a 30m burst window? Customer says it's a demo load and they may upgrade. If we need signoff, who should I loop in?

nora_billing: Looping in. Quick note: free-tier has a hard per-minute token cap and a small burst allowance (configured per-account). We can do a one-off exception for demos but need a purchaser's email and a quick PO/commit for anything >24h. For a 30-60 minute temporary exception I can authorize support to relax the limit to X3 for a single window if engineering confirms it's safe.

jon_engineer: @maria_support @nora_billing I looked at rate-limiter backends — free-tier safety gates are enforced by the token-bucket service. We can bump the bucket size for acct_9X7 for a single 60s window (e.g., allow an extra 120k tokens) but only if they accept a controlled timeout and we add a circuit-breaker to revert automatically. We need the request-id from one 429 to trace logs (acme provided req_83b9).

acme-devops: req_83b9 is the one. Appreciate it. If you can open a short window (30-60m) that'd be perfect. We'll apply client-side backoff right after and either throttle or upgrade.

maria_support: OK I'm initiating the escalation. Steps I'm taking: 1) open billing exception (nora) 2) ask eng to apply safe temporary bump (jon) 3) provide customer mitigation steps now so they stop wasting retries.

maria_support: Immediate mitigation for you to apply: lower concurrency to 2, add exponential backoff (start 500ms, multiply 2x), and reduce max_tokens to 256 for the next 10 minutes. Example pseudo: 
```js
async function sendWithBackoff(req) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < 6) {
    try {
      return await send(req);
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.status === 429) {
        await sleep(500 * Math.pow(2, attempt));
        attempt++;
        continue;
      }
      throw e;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('exhausted retries');
}
```

acme-devops: Doing that now — lowering tokens and concurrency. ETA 3m to flip.

nora_billing: Maria — authorized a 45-minute demo burst for acct_9X7 starting at 1791012380. Limits temporarily set to 3x baseline. Please confirm engineering will set an automated revert at 179101… (I put the end time to 1791012380 + 2700s).

jon_engineer: I applied a scoped bump at 1791012382 and added a revert job. I also added a temporary tracing rule to collect detailed rate limiter metrics for acct_9X7 for troubleshooting. watch for logs under request-id req_83b9 and subsequent ids.

support-bot: System notice — temporary rate-limit policy applied to acct_9X7 (policy_id: temp_burst_1791012382). Expires at 1791015082.

acme-devops: Confirmed — our retries dropped and 429s cleared. Thanks so much. We'll finalize upgrade decisions with sales.

maria_support: Great — @amy_sales, can you pick this up? Customer is on a demo, ready to discuss paid plans that remove these constraints. They asked about quick upgrade path and contract.

amy_sales: On it. I'll DM acme-devops and send a fast-quote for monthly committed capacity and Dedicated slots (if they prefer predictable throughput). For immediate path: credit-card self-serve upgrade will remove free-tier caps immediately; dedicated/reserved capacity provides SLAs.

maria_support: Closing the support side for now — leaving notes in the account: temporary-burst-granted, started 1791012382, expires 1791015082, traced by req_83b9. If anything spikes again, please reopen. :thumbsup:

acme-devops: Perfect. Thanks everyone — super fast turnaround.

jon_engineer: Follow-up action for eng: add a KB entry with the common repro (high-concurrency demo jobs) and recommended client mitigations (chunking, lower max_tokens, caching prefixes). I'll draft it this afternoon.

maria_support: Copy — I'll mark in the ticket and link the KB when ready.

nora_billing: Note for records: waived demo exception fee. If they convert to paid in 7 days, we'll apply a credit for the burst window.

acme-devops: Appreciate it. We'll follow up with sales within 48h.
